{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>At all times.</b> The early commentators explained it as concerning one matter, namely: if you know that Elohim desires your deeds, [then] \"eat your bread with joy,\" and you must [also] have your garments white — the meaning being that your deeds should not be fouled — and the meaning of \"let not oil be lacking from your head\" is that he should have a good name. And He commanded him to take a wife and not go astray with a stranger. And whatever your hand finds to do good in this world — do, for the world to come is not a world of deed but a world of recompense. But the correct interpretation is that the verses above and below are joined together, and it is \"the heart of the children of men\" speaking, as though one speaks with his own soul, and \"his heart\" is a circumlocution for his soul. And it is: eat and drink and clothe yourself in clean garments and anoint yourself constantly with oil and rejoice with the wife you desire, and all these are bodily pleasures alone."], :book :ecclesiastes, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 9, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [["Wikisource" "https://he.wikisource.org/wiki/%D7%90%D7%91%D7%9F_%D7%A2%D7%96%D7%A8%D7%90_%D7%A2%D7%9C_%D7%A7%D7%94%D7%9C%D7%AA"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:28:41.684035618Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}